.TH GREYWATCH 1 "November 2026" "greywatch 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
greywatch \- Linux endpoint monitoring and hardening suite
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B greywatch
(GUI, installed launcher: GreyWatch)
.br
.B gw
.IB command " [" arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
GreyWatch watches this machine's protections and tools from a desktop
dashboard (the GreyWatch app) and answers a text command line (gw).
Everything the GUI shows can be read the same way through gw, and the
two never disagree: both read the daemon's published status.
.PP
The daemon (greywatch.service) runs as the machine's root lane and
publishes status.json under /var/lib/greywatch; the desktop app and gw
read only what it publishes. Privileged work happens through the
apply/ helpers behind polkit. Nothing is ever changed without an
answer the operator clicked; the doctor proposes, it never auto-fixes.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B gw status
What the suite is doing right now.
.TP
.B gw events
The audit trail, newest first. Options pick tool and severity, and the
last N entries.
.TP
.B gw tail
Follow the audit trail live.
.TP
.B gw log TOOL
One tool's own log file. Only lines containing the text of the grep
option, or the last N lines.
.TP
.B gw decisions
List decision specs, all or pending only. Pending decisions are the
questions waiting on the operator; the dashboard counts this list.
.TP
.B gw answer ID OPTION
Answer a pending decision by its id and the option id to answer with.
.TP
.B gw action TOOL
Restart a tool, or manage offenders. See the doctor output for the
verbs.
.TP
.B gw doctor
Diagnose, recover, and verify the tools. The safe-repair flag applies
repairs, and the tool option scopes the run to one tool.
.PP
.B Do not confuse the doctor with the doctor machine mode:
the same command run as
.I doctor.py --machine
from the source tree prints one line
(DOCTOR n_red n_yellow) and exits 0 when healthy, 3 when something
reports a fault. Scripts and monitors use that.
.br
The separate machine-mode health lanes of the daemon itself (service,
scan, firewall, backlog, ledger) publish through status.json, so a monitor can
watch it without touching the process.
.TP
.B gw hunter
Threat hunts: show findings, or run one. The no-ai flag keeps the hunt
deterministic.
.TP
.B gw setup
First-install tool audit and setup.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/greywatch/status.json
The daemon's published face. The only window the desktop app and gw
look through.
.TP
.I /var/lib/greywatch/events.jsonl
The audit trail.
.TP
.I /var/lib/greywatch/decisions.jsonl
Operator decisions, append-only.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/greywatch.service
The daemon unit. Restart it after updates: a running daemon does not
pick up new code on its own (greywatch keeps the "never interrupt a
running model" law; a plain restart by the operator is the proven path).
.SH SEE ALSO
systemctl(1), nft(8), psad(8)
